**Docker**

The image runs as uid 1000, so create its two folders owned by that uid first. Then pull and start it
with the attached `docker-compose.yml`, setting `BLUETOOTH_GID` in `.env`:

```
printf 'BLUETOOTH_GID=%s\n' "$(getent group bluetooth | cut -d: -f3)" >> .env
mkdir -p config run && sudo chown 1000:1000 config run
docker pull ghcr.io/rralex/svolder:@VERSION@
docker compose up -d
```

The computer's volume and EQ are opt-in. Save the attached `docker-compose.host-audio.yml` as
`docker-compose.override.yml` beside `docker-compose.yml` and append your user id and runtime
directory to `.env` (`>>`, never `>`):

```
printf 'HOST_UID=%s\nHOST_RUNTIME_DIR=%s\n' "$(id -u)" "${XDG_RUNTIME_DIR:-/run/user/$(id -u)}" >> .env
```

The override mounts your whole runtime directory (`/run/user/<uid>`) read-only: the container can
connect to every socket in it, the session bus and agent sockets included, which a single-socket
mount would avoid but which goes stale when PipeWire restarts. With it, the container runs as your
user id, so create `config` and `run` owned by you (`sudo chown $(id -u) config run`) instead of
1000, and files it writes there carry group 0 on the host. To turn it off, delete the override file.

To update, `docker compose pull` and then `docker compose up -d`. The image is built for x86_64 and arm64. The attached `docker-compose.yml` and `docker-compose.host-audio.yml` are the two files here
`SHA256SUMS` does not list.
